WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.800
A prayer that's way more than just a prayer.&nbsp;
A mysterious dragon "too big for this world."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.428
"House of the Dragon" is heating up — here's&nbsp;
what you may have missed in Episode 2.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.760
Episode 2 begins with some gnarly&nbsp;
images of sea life cleaning the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.680
flesh off the bodies of sailors&nbsp;
who appear to have washed ashore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.840
In the next scene, a furious Corlys Velaryon&nbsp;
bursts into a small council meeting and says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.240
something must be done about the Stepstones,&nbsp;
the Free Cities, and the Crabfeeder.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.680
"Four ships have now been lost!&nbsp;
The last one was flying my banner."

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:39.200
Otto Hightower's solution is to pay off the&nbsp;
families of the victims and refund Corlys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.160
for his losses, but the Sea Snake doesn't&nbsp;
think this course of action goes far enough.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.440
In the premiere, Corlys speaks of the&nbsp;
Triarchy and someone named Craghas Drahar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.760
In Episode 2, when Corlys asks Daemon&nbsp;
if he's heard about the "trouble in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.280
the Stepstones" in an attempt to&nbsp;
recruit him into the war he wants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.760
Daemon replies that he has heard something about&nbsp;
some Myrish prince. Craghas, the Crabfeeder, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:09.920
this prince are one and the same, and the Triarchy&nbsp;
is the Free Cities of Lys, Myr, and Tyrosh.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.040
The Crabfeeder came by his nickname honestly.&nbsp;
Feeding sailors to the crabs might seem like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.880
metaphor for sea warfare, but Craghas literally&nbsp;
feeds his victims to his namesakes by fixing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.400
their bodies to stakes at low tide and letting&nbsp;
the crabs do their thing. The source material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.160
George R.R. Martin's "Fire &amp; Blood," leaves&nbsp;
more to the imagination and simply explains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.480
that the name was related to the inventive&nbsp;
punishments he inflicted upon his enemies.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:44.080
History buff King Viserys is building (or at least&nbsp;
commissioning) an elaborate model of Valyria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.640
the home of the Targaryens and the other&nbsp;
now-extinct dragon-riding families.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.800
"The city of 1000 years, and&nbsp;
all that men had learned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.520
The doom consumed it all alike.&nbsp;
And neither of them turned."

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.480
During one of their visits in Episode 2, he&nbsp;
explains his project to the young lady who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:08.000
obviously been sent to seduce him, Alicent&nbsp;
Hightower, daughter of the Hand of the King.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.480
Viserys waxes poetic about how&nbsp;
Valyria was built into a volcano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.120
like Dragonstone, so its people could be&nbsp;
close to the source of their magic and power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.320
He tells of blood mages and a civilization&nbsp;
that was 1,000 dragons strong.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.120
"Do you believe that Westeros could&nbsp;
be another Valryia, your grace?"

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:30.640
The king answers that it depends on whether&nbsp;
she means Valyria at its peak or at its fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.040
referring to the Doom of Valyria. Though&nbsp;
Martin has not yet written a definitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.840
account of exactly what happened, Valyria&nbsp;
suffered some apocalyptic event about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.960
dozen years after the Targaryens fled, thanks&nbsp;
to the prophetic vision of Daenys Targaryen.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.080
Some characters speak of the Doom as if&nbsp;
it was an unavoidable natural disaster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.760
but the strong implication is that&nbsp;
the Valyrians brought collapse upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.480
themselves with their lust for magic and power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.400
Viserys considers his own kingdom with 10 dragons,&nbsp;
already falling into disrepair after 100 years.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.280
"The glory of Old Valyria&nbsp;
will never be seen again."

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.960
Maybe not the glory, but perhaps the hubris&nbsp;
and ruin. Viserys thinks ruling over peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.920
means throwing feasts and tourneys, not&nbsp;
making difficult but necessary decisions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.880
Meanwhile, the seven hells&nbsp;
are about to break loose.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.560
It's incredibly symbolic when he&nbsp;
drops and breaks a dragon figurine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.800
and mistakes Alicent's ambition&nbsp;
for kindness when she has it fixed.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:34.056
"For thousands of years the Valyrians were best&nbsp;
in the world at almost everything. And then…”

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:35.674
“And then they weren’t."

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.520
Episode 2 makes it clear that&nbsp;
Alicent Hightower is a willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.120
participant and not just a pawn in&nbsp;
the schemes that revolve around her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.520
though the nervous fingernail picking&nbsp;
suggests she certainly has mixed feelings.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.320
The main plotline revolves around Alicent&nbsp;
and her father Otto's efforts to convince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.040
Rhaenyra that her father the king should&nbsp;
remarry, and to make sure he chooses the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:02.960
teenage Alicent over the 12-year-old Laena&nbsp;
Velaryon, who's the better candidate on paper.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.160
To soften up her bestie, Alicent has to&nbsp;
mediate the reconciliation between Rhaenyra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:13.840
and her father, who've barely spoken since her&nbsp;
mother and brother's traumatic deaths. Step one:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.280
Alicent takes her to the Sept to pray to the&nbsp;
Seven in an attempt to get some closure about it.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:24.560
This scene is much more loaded with subtext and&nbsp;
foreshadowing than it might seem upon first watch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.720
The Hightowers are the lords of Oldtown,&nbsp;
where they were once the richest and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:33.200
powerful family in Westeros. The Faith&nbsp;
of the Seven — the predominant religion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:38.960
in Westeros — also calls Oldtown home,&nbsp;
and the two entities are closely aligned.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.960
The Hightowers support the Faith and&nbsp;
vice versa, which increases both the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:48.160
noble family and the new religion's political&nbsp;
influence. In contrast, the Targaryens have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.440
tenuous relationship with the Seven. Publicly,&nbsp;
they've converted, but really, they keep their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.160
ancient customs and do their best to limit&nbsp;
the Seven's role in the culture of Westeros.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.600
By getting Rhaenyra to bend the knee in prayer,&nbsp;
Alicent is flexing her own strength and using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.720
the religion to which she's theoretically much&nbsp;
more devoted than Rhaenyra for cynical purposes.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.302
"What do I say?"

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:12.753
"Whatever you wish.

00:05:14.262 --> 00:05:16.267
It's only&nbsp;
for you and the gods to know." 

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.120
Showrunners have promised that we'll see at least&nbsp;
17 dragons throughout the run of "House of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.880
Dragon," including at least 9 in the first season.&nbsp;
So far, we've gotten glimpses of Rhaenyra's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:33.920
elegant mount, Syrax, and Daemon's fierce red&nbsp;
steed, Caraxes, also known as the Blood Wyrm.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:39.120
We've also heard about Balerion, the Black Dread,&nbsp;
whose skull now rests in the cellars of the Red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:45.040
Keep, and Dreamfyre, whose egg was chosen for&nbsp;
Rhaenyra's brother, then stolen by Daemon.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:51.040
On his walk with Laena Velaryon, the tween&nbsp;
Princess asks the King what it was like to ride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:57.600
Balerion. Some backstory: Viserys was the last&nbsp;
of the Targaryen kings to ride the Black Dread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.040
and he never bonded with or&nbsp;
rode another dragon afterwards.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:04.800
That means it's been quite some time&nbsp;
since the King was on dragonback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:12.160
which is itself a symbol of his supposed weakness.&nbsp;
Then, Laena inquires about Vhagar who she says was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:17.680
too big for the dragonpit. Viserys retorts&nbsp;
that she was perhaps too big for this world.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:23.360
Vhagar was a female dragon, almost as large as&nbsp;
Balerion, who was ridden by one of King Aegon's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:29.280
sister-wives, Visenya. Long story short, Vhagar&nbsp;
— the biggest dragon in the world — is still out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.640
there, and no one knows for sure where she's&nbsp;
nesting. This conversation isn't just scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:40.960
filler. Vhagar is basically Chekhov's dragon, sure&nbsp;
to show up at some critical moment in the future.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:47.120
After King Viserys slices his finger on the&nbsp;
Iron Throne in Episode 1, we see in Episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:52.720
2 that what started out as a small cut has&nbsp;
rotted into a serious health situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.600
Though it occurs at a different point in the&nbsp;
narrative, in "Fire &amp; Blood," Viserys cuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:03.600
his digit so badly that it needs to be amputated.&nbsp;
The incident scars him, physically and mentally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.640
so much so that, in the book, King Viserys&nbsp;
never sits on the Iron Throne again.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:14.320
In "House of the Dragon," the Maesters recommend&nbsp;
maggots to eat away at the gangrene. Both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:19.120
versions come to the same metaphorical&nbsp;
conclusion: the wound will not heal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.400
Viserys himself utters this exact phrase&nbsp;
in another context, later in the episode.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.800
"Your mother's absence is a&nbsp;
wound that will never heal."

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:34.960
He adds that in Queen Aemma's absence, the Red&nbsp;
Keep "will never recover," and he's right. Had she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:40.000
lived, Viserys would've remained married to her&nbsp;
and probably wouldn't have produced another heir.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:45.440
Rhaenyra's claim would be absolute, save&nbsp;
for Daemon's insolence at being passed over.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.400
With Aemma out of the picture, King&nbsp;
Viserys has to choose between two factions:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.600
one led by the Hightowers and one led by&nbsp;
the Velaryons. Since neither side will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.808
ever accept the promotion of the other over&nbsp;
their own interests, civil war is inevitable.

